 that that sessions took  sessions uh  every other month would go back to texas  to his home state and he'd make up some  some excuse like he's going to talk to a  rotary club and then he'd go to san  francisco to see his daughter he'd go  to atlantic city to see a ballet and all  this was a taxpayer expense one thousand  dollars an hour is what the cost of fbi  plane is but that was only part of the  abuses  i mentioned the sarah munford abuses  that he condone  he also gave an fbi building passed to  his wife alice when you have to have a  top secret security clearance to have  that passed by federal regulation so  members of congress and white house  aides and journalists who come to the  fbi building have to all be signed in  and escorted up but but only  his wife alice was allowed to just walk  in at will with this pass if fbi agents  forget their pass uh you know their  their their discipline their there's a  letter in their file etc and yet uh  she was able to to you know have this  privilege uh  just because she was the director's wife  uh there was also a sweetheart mortgage  on his home that came out later uh  because he was fbi director he was given  uh a special deal on the on the interest  rate and the and the points by riggs  national bank because sessions had  talked to the  uh  the chairman of the board of riggs  joseph  right how do you know this this was uh  detailed in the in the opr report the  office of professional responsibility uh  and at at first sessions wouldn't even  provide the mortgage documents  uh for the investigators you know which  is which is sort of the attack taken by  people who are being investigated by the  fbi  uh and then when it all  came out and the report came out  sessions  claimed that he had never been given a  chance to give his side which was untrue  it's been four days being interviewed by  the opr people and also i've seen the  response that he's given to the opr  report which is a very detailed  line-by-line  rebuttal which which is totally  ineffective but but he uh you know when  he said that he had never had a chance  to to actually respond  that was another disingenuous response  what was your relationship with him and  mrs session  from the beginning how did what happened  to that relationship  when i first went into it i you know i  had this image of that most people had i  think of of this former federal judge  who probably was something like william  webster the the other director who had  been a judge before him  and then when i met him you know he  seemed extremely affable uh you know  very very  nice kind of person  uh  and uh as i got into these abuses you  know of course i was very puzzled and  and tried to uh  figure it out in my mind uh how could  this person who's so affable and and  and seemed so caring uh and stood for uh  equality uh also be so blind to to the  fact that he was engaging in wrongdoing  and  what i started to learn was that he was  also rather arrogant he would he would  for example when being briefed by fbi  agents about very uh sensitive  investigations he would just  start singing the brill cream commercial  for example  that's just one you know quirky example  and then when he was introduced to a  chinese diplomat  he said there was no need for an  interpreter  he speaks chinese and then he started  saying chop suey chow mein you know i  think that that indicates arrogance and  and there was a certain nastiness to him  i thought because  when he finally did  respond to my letter outlining these  abuses and he wouldn't comment on them  or or answer any questions but he he  bought me out for looking into these  matters at all or any embarrassing  matters at the fbi uh he  started  comparing himself with some of the other  directors and he said  that uh other directors wives had been  sick or or had died  that william webster's wife drew you  know had died well she died you know  what  he apparently was was criticizing  webster because his wife had died and  and and clarence kelly uh the other  director because his wife was sick you  know in fact webster's wife drew uh was  dearly loved by the fbi uh and respected  by the fbi and and uh had been uh was  alive for the first six years of of  webster's term and and so  i think there was a nastiness to to his  um  his outlook uh  which was compounded by his wife alice  who had this very paranoid view of  almost anything you know people were out  to get her in fact she told me that  the fbi was was bugging her  and  the fbi could do nothing right uh why  why would she feel that way  i i think it it has to do with her her  uh her just her mindset um  that that's the kind of personality she  she is  she would imagine all kinds of things  for example she  kept saying and sessions kept  apparently ratifying  that ron mccall this low-level security  agent had been found by her in their  bedroom and without their permission you  know and that was just a total  fabrication it never happened uh the the  fbi  uh internal investigators looked into  that and they found that there was a  previous incident involving his his  predecessor who had gone to the house  with sessions permission  to change the code on the two-way radio  in his bedroom and she came home and saw  him and uh and he said you know the  sessions that said  it's okay and and that was it but she  made it made it into this great  conspiracy involving ron mccall who had  never done anything wrong except to  question some of the  uh abuses that she  tried to get the fbi to engage in one of  the things that's difficult is that mr  sessions isn't here to defend himself  and neither is she did you try at any  point  to get them to talk to you about these  abuses  these alleged abuses they still have not  been charged as far as i know  well you know it's not a criminal matter  i mean there are a range of there's a  range of wrongdoing and abuse that can  occur without it being a criminal case  and in this case uh the office of  professional responsibility of the  justice department wrote a report which  which confirmed these matters and found  that they were even more serious than i  thought  and uh when  the pres when the president fired him  he  and janet reno said that  they had found that based on this report  that he had a serious problem with  judgment and that therefore he was being  dismissed  um has he ever told his side of the  story oh yeah he's been on tv uh you  know on radio and and and been  interviewed  uh constantly and then does he answer  the specific charges  well he's he's addressed them he's been  asked about them i don't think you know  in my opinion that he actually has has  answered them uh honestly he's  come up with all these  excuses he's claimed that it was a great  cabal that was out to get him it was  either the democrats  or the republicans or it was hoover  rights and the fbi or bigots or  or me who was being used as a as a dupe  and a pawn of these hoover rights  and it's all  fabrication what'd they do when he left  when he left i mean what's he doing now  um i don't know i don't you know i think  he's looking for a job basically  normally by the way how old a man is he  uh  perhaps  probably ryan is i think he might be  around 60 roughly  here's what the book looks like  and i want to show once we see the cover  on the screen i want to open it up so  you can  see inside this picture normally it  authors tell you that it takes nine  months to get a book  printed after you've finished it and  here you've already got a picture of the  new fbi director who just  recently took the oath how did you get  it in here so fast  i had really finished the manuscript a  year before and but then i kept updating  it and adding to it quite extensively  including  5000 words on the waco incident alone uh  and then it was already in bound galley  form when clinton fired the director  so at that point the publisher  pocketbooks decided to  crash it and