y cared because they didn't have a  great name  you know deep throat wasn't very  important but but there are other  important sources as well you dedicate  the book for pam greg and rachel kessler  who are they  my wife pam and my daughter rachel and  my son greg how old are they not your  wife necessarily but how are the kids  my son is 26 he's an artist in new york  and my daughter rachel is 24 and she's  at the wall street journal washington  bureau doing what she's a news assistant  and writing stories on the side as well  and hoping to be a reporter does your  wife work  she is an author also she's a former  washington post reporter now writing  books she wrote a book about the spy  sites of washington and she's written a  novel now and pocket books who are they  that's part of simon schuster  they like to be identified as just  pocket books not simon schuster because  they're almost in competition with each  other but  uh  my editor there is paul mccarthy who  really had a lot to do with  getting this book out  and  making it what it is in the back you  have 504  notes um you did it in kind of a  different way i mean you actually go  from one to 504 instead of doing it by  chapter was that your idea  uh yeah and you know with word perfect  it's much easier to just go  like that chronologically  and i think it's easier for people to  pick out the notes that way how many  interviews did you do for this book  there were 314 people interviewed mostly  agents who were current some former  agents some other employees as well did  you interview them on audio tape  yes everyone except for a few very  sensitive ones were tape recorded with  people's permission what do you do with  the tape  that's in a bank fault what are you  going to do with that  i usually keep it for several years and  then  and then re-record for the next book is  there another book underway already  yes but if you don't mind i'd like to  keep that quiet it's about another  very secretive very powerful institution  uh and it'll be out next year  and uh  investigative reporting obviously is  your thing  you have any idea where you first got  interested in that part of journalism  investigative reporting  i i recall uh you know my  temple newspaper uh  doing a story about um the blue laws of  massachusetts and and quoting  a number of clergymen as saying that  even they didn't think that they were  necessary  and i remember that as being  something that i liked to do because  it contributed to public understanding  perhaps  would lead to some reforms uh  got to the heart of the  of the truth in this particular case and  so that was a very minor example and  then when i was on my college paper  clark  i did a story  uh about uh discrimination against  blacks they were called negros in those  days  in rental housing and to do that i would  call various people who would advertise  their homes for for rent and i would say  is it still open they would say yes and  i would say by the way my roommate is  black is that a problem and 60 said yes  that would be a problem  and that led to quite a few  investigations in massachusetts and  so  things like that  sort of wetted my appetite i like to  get involved in controversial things but  but more so to get to the truth one of  the things that i uh you know new thing  that i learned in here and explain it  was that  if you're an agent and you're gaining  access to i'm not sure what building or  what place you look into something and  they read your eyes what's that uh it's  an optical  uh  scanner  which detects the pattern of  blood vessels in your eye  i guess in the retina or some place and  each person has a unique pattern  so they record that in the computer and  then  in order to gain access to the building  you look into a little  uh  optical piece which  senses the same pattern and if if you're  the same person it lets you in  there's a lot more and we only have a  minute can you give us  to finalize this  your a